WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:03.620
We know gravity is very weird in several ways.

00:00:03.620 --> 00:00:07.049
It's also the force we understand the least
in some sense.

00:00:07.049 --> 00:00:08.880
It's weird because it's incredibly weak.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:14.080
For example, if you take the attractive force
— the gravitational force between an electron

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:18.790
and a proton in a hydrogen atom — it's something
like a billion billion billion billion times

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:22.480
weaker than the electromagnetic force that
holds the atom together.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:27.609
So gravity's really incredibly weak, and it's
weak in a way that we don't understand.

00:00:27.609 --> 00:00:31.609
The other odd feature of gravity is that unlike
the other forces we know about, which we can

00:00:31.609 --> 00:00:36.550
understand at microscopic distances with quantum
mechanics, for gravity, our best theory comes

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.469
from Einstein's theory of general relativity.

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:40.809
And that explains gravity at very long distances.

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:45.570
But we still don't know how to actually take
Einstein's theory of relativity and combine

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.920
it with quantum mechanics to understand fundamental
gravitational interactions at very short distances.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:57.379
So the third thing we actually know is that
over the past few decades, astrophysical observations

00:00:57.379 --> 00:01:01.179
have told us that the vast majority of the
energy density, or the stuff that's actually

00:01:01.179 --> 00:01:05.810
in the universe, consists of these mysterious
components that we call dark matter and dark

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:06.810
energy.

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:11.479
And we really don't know what these are, but
all we really know from these observations

00:01:11.479 --> 00:01:13.690
is that they have to interact gravitationally.

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.390
So it really seems like gravity is at the
heart of some of these major unanswered questions

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.390
in physics.

00:01:18.390 --> 00:01:23.500
A better understanding of gravity may be able
to help us start to answer some of these questions.

